      ],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": "5f67fd3b03061d2721edc959",
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5f673d0011ffdc734dbb6af8",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": {
        "like": 8
      },
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5a9de400f3791b4155afcc5b|full_name}: Rankings only loosely correlate with performance. The ranking system cannot judge things in context. \nIt can't tell the difference between an avatar with switchable items and an avatar with several extraneous parts. It can't tell cheap particle systems from expensive ones. It can't tell vertex lights from pixel lights. It doesn't even attempt to measure shapekeys.\nIt also can't judge things that have different costs on different configurations.\nNow, it's infeasible to try and have the system judge the cost of these things. But that means that like any automatic metric system, it can't be blindly trusted.\nIt's easy to assume that things would just be better if everyone stayed within the ranks, but that would restrict people's creativity... and the effectiveness would be questionable except in the most tightly controlled circumstances. It is often the case that an instance will slow down once filled with people, but just hiding avatars won't speed it back up again. \nAll it would do is push people into doing things *because the ranking system won't penalize them*. For the above reasons, that would be quite dangerous.\n(Sidenote: Particle system anecdotes. Someone can destroy your framerate by dropping 10-20 big smoke particles. But  40,000 swirling danmaku bullets filling the sky is pretty cheap. There are good reasons why this is so, but the system can't account for them.)",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
